@page "/projects/between-bells"
@layout ParkerPortfolio.Layouts.ProjectLayout

<PageTitle>Between Bells · Aaron Joel Parker</PageTitle>

<div class="canvas">
    <a class="back" href="/">&larr; Portfolio</a>

    <section class="hero">
        <p class="eyebrow">The @P.Archetype</p>
        <h1 class="title">@P.Name</h1>
        <p class="tagline">@P.Tagline</p>
        <p class="status">@P.Status</p>
    </section>

    <section class="prose">
        <h2>The premise</h2>
        <p>
            A watchmaker, in love with the princess, slips out at curfew. Between one
            bell and the next he must cross courtyards, scale a trellis, and reach her
            balcony — past royal guards who do not know him and would not forgive him
            if they did. He carries no blade. Drawing one ends the game.
        </p>

        <h2>The technology</h2>
        <p>
            Between Bells is a medieval HD-2D stealth game on a custom .NET 10 engine.
            Silk.NET binds OpenGL 4.1; GLSL handles the parallax and bloom that give
            the look its depth. ImGui drives the development overlays. The engine and
            the game live in separate clean-architecture layers — moonlight on stone,
            but well-organized underneath.
        </p>

        <h2>What it shows</h2>
        <p>
            That a senior .NET engineer can step outside ASP.NET Core and build
            something that has to render at sixty frames per second without ever
            forgetting why a clean seam between engine and game matters.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="stack">
        <p class="stack-label">Stack</p>
        <div class="chips">
            @foreach (var tech in P.Stack)
            {
                <span class="chip">@tech</span>
            }
        </div>
    </section>
</div>

@code {
    private static readonly Project P = ProjectsCatalog.All.First(p => p.Slug == "between-bells");
}
